Pairwise (Head-to-Head Comparison)
The judge sees two responses to the same prompt and picks the better one.
Prompt template:
Compare Response A and Response B to the following query.

Query: {query}
Response A: {response_a}
Response B: {response_b}

Which response is better? Consider: helpfulness, accuracy, completeness, clarity.
First explain your reasoning, then state your final verdict: [[A]] or [[B]] or [[tie]]

Listwise (Ranking)
The judge sees N responses and ranks them all.
Prompt template:
Rank the following responses to the query from best to worst.
Query: {query}
Response 1: {response_1}
Response 2: {response_2}
...
Response N: {response_n}
Consider helpfulness, accuracy, completeness, clarity. First explain your reasoning, then provide a final ranking.

Step 5: Build LLM-as-a-Judge, Iteratively
Take the expert’s critiques and use them to build a judge:
System prompt: "You are a quality evaluator for [application]. 
Judge outputs as PASS or FAIL based on these criteria: [rubric derived from critiques]."

Few-shot examples: 3–5 (trace, judgment, critique) tuples from the expert

Detailed Example : a text-to-SQL evaluator (pattern from Hamel’s query evaluator)
You are a SQL query evaluator. You are given a natural-language question and the SQL a
model generated for it. Judge whether the SQL correctly answers the question asked.

Schema:
  customers(customer_id PK, name, country, created_at)
  orders(order_id PK, customer_id FK, status, total_amount, created_at)
  order_items(order_item_id PK, order_id FK, product_id, quantity, unit_price)

Guidelines:
- A query that RUNS is not the same as a query that is CORRECT.
- Check the grain: does joining a one-to-many table duplicate rows before an aggregate?
- Check NULL semantics, especially NOT IN against a nullable column.
- Check that filters and date ranges match what was asked, with no off-by-one.
- Prefer the simplest query that answers the question.

Example evaluations:

&lt;examples&gt;

&lt;example-1&gt;
&lt;question&gt;How many orders were placed last month?&lt;/question&gt;
&lt;sql&gt;
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders
WHERE created_at &gt;= date_trunc('month', current_date - interval '1 month')
  AND created_at &lt;  date_trunc('month', current_date);
&lt;/sql&gt;
&lt;critique&gt;
{
  "critique": "Correct. The range is half-open — inclusive of the first instant of last
   month, exclusive of the first instant of this month — so no row is double counted and
   no row at a month boundary is missed. COUNT(*) is right because the question asks for
   a number of orders, not distinct customers.",
  "outcome": "good"
}
&lt;/critique&gt;
&lt;/example-1&gt;

&lt;example-2&gt;
&lt;question&gt;What is the total revenue per customer?&lt;/question&gt;
&lt;sql&gt;
SELECT c.name, SUM(o.total_amount) AS revenue
FROM customers c
JOIN orders o      ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id
JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id  = o.order_id
GROUP BY c.name;
&lt;/sql&gt;
&lt;critique&gt;
{
  "critique": "Wrong, and silently so. Joining order_items fans each order out into one row
   per line item, so total_amount is summed once per item: an order of 100 with three items
   contributes 300. The query runs and returns plausible-looking numbers. order_items is not
   needed at all here. Grouping by name rather than customer_id also merges distinct
   customers who share a name.",
  "outcome": "bad"
}
&lt;/critique&gt;
&lt;/example-2&gt;

&lt;example-3&gt;
&lt;question&gt;List customers who have never placed an order.&lt;/question&gt;
&lt;sql&gt;
SELECT name FROM customers
WHERE customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders);
&lt;/sql&gt;
&lt;critique&gt;
{
  "critique": "Wrong whenever orders.customer_id contains a NULL. NOT IN evaluates to NULL
   rather than TRUE once the subquery yields a NULL, so the predicate is never satisfied and
   the query returns ZERO rows — not an error, just a silently empty result. Use NOT EXISTS,
   or filter the subquery with WHERE customer_id IS NOT NULL.",
  "outcome": "bad"
}
&lt;/critique&gt;
&lt;/example-3&gt;

&lt;/examples&gt;

For the following query, first write a detailed critique explaining your reasoning,
then give a good/bad judgment in the same format as above.

&lt;question&gt;{{user_question}}&lt;/question&gt;
&lt;sql&gt;
{{generated_sql}}
&lt;/sql&gt;
&lt;critique&gt;

Relative Position and Rotation
Moving \(k\) steps forward is exactly a rotation by \(\omega k\):




\[
\begin{pmatrix}\sin(\omega(p{+}k))\\ \cos(\omega(p{+}k))\end{pmatrix}
=
\underbrace{\begin{pmatrix}\cos(\omega k) & \sin(\omega k)\\ -\sin(\omega k) & \cos(\omega k)\end{pmatrix}}_{\text{rotation by }\omega k}
\begin{pmatrix}\sin(\omega p)\\ \cos(\omega p)\end{pmatrix}
\]

The rotation depends only on the shift \(k\), never on the absolute position \(p\).
So relative distance is, in principle, recoverable from the absolute encoding.

Multi-Head Attention : Naive Implementation

# naive version using loops

class MultiHeadAttentionWrapper(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, d_in, d_out, context_length, dropout, num_heads, qkv_bias=False):
        super().__init__()
        self.heads = nn.ModuleList([
            CausalAttention(d_in, d_out, context_length, dropout, qkv_bias)
            for _ in range(num_heads)
        ])

    def forward(self, x):
        return torch.cat([head(x) for head in self.heads], dim=-1)

Connection : server/discover
Servers MUST implement server/discover. It advertises supported protocol versions, capabilities, and identity.

Clients MAY call it before anything else, for up-front version selection
Or use it as a backward-compatibility probe on STDIO
